    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse a DP table where dp[i][j] is the minimum edits to convert the first i characters of one string into the first j of another. The transitions represent insert, delete, and replace.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is a common coding question because it reveals whether you can choose an efficient approach and explain why it is correct. The interviewer can then probe complexity, invariants, and edge cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem precisely.\n2. Explain the data structure, invariant, or recurrence that drives the solution.\n3. Give the time and space complexity.\n4. Mention the edge case that changes the implementation, such as duplicates, empty input, negative values, or an even/odd boundary.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would state the approach before writing code, then explain the invariant that keeps it correct. After that I would give the complexity and walk through one small example. If the interviewer changes a constraint, I would explain how that changes the solution.\n\n**Example :**\nConverting \"kitten\" to \"sitting\" takes 3 edits: replace k->s, replace e->i, and append g.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nThe metric is useful for spell checking, fuzzy matching, and comparing text values.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Coding before explaining the approach or invariant.\n- Quoting the wrong complexity.\n- Missing the constraint that makes the chosen data structure or algorithm necessary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
