    focus: "Java, SQL, cloud, APIs, microservices, DSA, testing, and client solution delivery",
    question: "Process vs Thread",
    slug: "capgemini-process-vs-thread-71",
    shortDescription: "Compare processes and threads.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA process has its own virtual address space and operating-system resources; threads are execution units within a process and normally share that process memory.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you understand the protocol, operating-system, or security boundary rather than only knowing the terminology. The interviewer is usually looking for flow, failure behavior, and the reason for the guarantee.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the protocol or system behavior.\n2. Trace the relevant flow or state transition.\n3. State the guarantee and what it does not guarantee.\n4. Mention the failure or security consequence that matters.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would start with the boundary or protocol definition, then walk through the flow. After that I would state what guarantee the mechanism provides and one important limitation or failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nTwo threads in one server process can share in-memory caches, while two processes need an IPC mechanism or external store to share data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nThreads are cheaper for shared-memory concurrency, while processes provide stronger isolation at the cost of more overhead.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing layers, such as transport behavior with application behavior.\n- Treating a security control as a complete security solution.\n- Ignoring failure, timing, or concurrency.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA process has its own virtual address space and operating-system resources; threads are execution units within a process and normally share that process memory.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you understand the protocol, operating-system, or security boundary rather than only knowing the terminology. The interviewer is usually looking for flow, failure behavior, and the reason for the guarantee.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the protocol or system behavior.\n2. Trace the relevant flow or state transition.\n3. State the guarantee and what it does not guarantee.\n4. Mention the failure or security consequence that matters.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would start with the boundary or protocol definition, then walk through the flow. After that I would state what guarantee the mechanism provides and one important limitation or failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nTwo threads in one server process can share in-memory caches, while two processes need an IPC mechanism or external store to share data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nThreads are cheaper for shared-memory concurrency, while processes provide stronger isolation at the cost of more overhead.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing layers, such as transport behavior with application behavior.\n- Treating a security control as a complete security solution.\n- Ignoring failure, timing, or concurrency.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
    explanation: "This OS, Networking & Security question is included in Capgemini software-engineer interview preparation. It evaluates practical reasoning, correctness, trade-offs, and communication.",
